<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abdallah al-Mahdi Billah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">proclaimed caliph <a href="/years/909">909</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abdallah al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> Billah was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a>, proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> in 909 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>. An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> who claimed descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatima">Fatima</a>, the Prophet's daughter, he came to power after the missionary campaign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-abdallah-al-shii">Abu Abdallah al-Shi'i</a> among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutama-berbers">Kutama Berbers</a> overthrew the Aghlabid rulers. His proclamation established a Shia caliphate in direct rivalry with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abdallah-al-mahdi-billah.jpg" alt="Abdallah al-Mahdi Billah" width="320" height="319" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PHGCOM · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Calif_al_Mahdi_Kairouan_912_CE(png).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The origin and early history of the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> are obscure, and even his name and birthplace are disputed. According to his official biography he was born in Askar Mukram in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzistan</a> on 31 July 874; other traditions give <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> or Salamiya. His name was most likely Sa'id ibn al-Husayn. His father died in 881/2, and he was fostered by his uncle at Salamiya, the base of the family's secret Isma'ili missionary network, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawa">da'wa</a>.</p><p>In the mid-890s Sa'id took over the leadership of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawa">da'wa</a>. His claim that he and his ancestors held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">imamate</a> themselves, rather than acting as trustees of a hidden imam, led to a schism in 899, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarmatians">Qarmatians</a> split off. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> uprising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in 902-903 drew the attention of the Abbasid authorities to him. He fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramla">Ramla</a>, then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and finally to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sijilmasa">Sijilmasa</a>, where he lived as a merchant until his missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-abdallah-al-shii">Abu Abdallah al-Shi'i</a> led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutama-berbers">Kutama Berbers</a> to overthrow the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabid dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Abu Abdallah, who ran the new regime while his master was still in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sijilmasa">Sijilmasa</a>, entered the Aghlabid palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raqqada">Raqqada</a> on 25 March 909. Once al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> had taken power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> he claimed the right to conquer the world in the name of God. He soon fell out with Abu Abdallah and other leading missionaries, who were disappointed that he was not the semi-divine mahdi they had been propagating for, and he purged them. He also had to overcome revolts, some against the exactions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutama-berbers">Kutama</a>, who were the backbone of his power.</p><p>The state he built was underpinned by a messianic ideology but was otherwise conventionally organised, and relied on personnel of the previous Aghlabid regime. Two invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> were beaten back by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>, leaving only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a> in his hands, and the war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> brought raids for plunder and slaves without lasting success. In the west the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> challenged his attempts to rule the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a>. In 921 he moved his court to the newly built palace city of Mahdiya on the Tunisian coast.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died in 934 and was succeeded by his only son, al-Qa'im. Whether his claims of descent from Ali were true has been debated since the early 900s and has no definite answer. Marius Canard wrote that, whoever he may have been, he laid the foundations of the dynasty in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. Michael Brett points out that building a state left him little time to develop Isma'ili doctrine into a full creed of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">imamate</a>, which was achieved under the fourth Fatimid imam-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, al-Mu'izz.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abd Allah al-Mahdi Billah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 909 his Kutama army overthrew the Aghlabid emirate, and the imam was retrieved from Sijilmasa and proclaimed caliph at Raqqada in January 910 as <b>Abdallah al-Mahdi Billah</b>, establishing a rival caliphate to Baghdad.</blockquote><a href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Between about 905 and 909 the future Fatimid caliph <b>Ubayd Allah</b> was held captive there until rescued by Abu Abdallah al-Shi'i, who executed the emir al-Yasa.</blockquote><a href="/state/midrarid-emirate">Midrarid emirate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in ʻAskar Makram. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdia">Mahdia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.51,11.06&amp;year=909" aria-label="Mahdia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Abdallah al-Mahdi Billah"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5765 4493h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5765 4493h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=35.51,11.06&amp;year=909">What was here in 909 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
